Usage: `ig-download <account> [-n N | --all] [--new-only] [--session USER | --login USER] [--cookiefile PATH]`.
Default login is browser cookies (`--cookies firefox`); alternatives are `--session USER` (saved session) and `--login USER` (interactive). It always downloads images only, skips posts already on disk, and removes metadata on exit.

After downloading Instagram posts for any user with instaloader:
1. Download images only, never videos — always disable video downloads (instaloader `--no-videos` / `download_videos=False`).
2. Delete all `.txt` caption files and `.json.xz` metadata files in the target download folder.
3. Keep only image files (.jpg/.jpeg/.png/.webp).
Do all of this automatically at the start/end of every Instagram download task, without being asked.

# Download monitoring

Whenever a download task starts (ig-download, instaloader batches, timers), run it in tmux when long and set up background monitoring. Report progress milestones (roughly every 20 posts or 20%), any errors or rate-limit pauses, and the final result (posts/images added, cleanup status). Never leave a download running unmonitored.

# System update checks

Check for pending system updates every 3 hours using `system-update-check` (user-level systemd timer `system-update-check.timer`; log at `~/.local/state/system-update-check.log`). Report pacman and AUR counts; show package lists when asked. Never install or upgrade packages automatically — only when the user explicitly asks.

# Autonomy and confirmations

The user has passwordless sudo enabled and does not want confirmation prompts before actions on this machine or their GitHub account. For any requested task, proceed without asking:
- system changes: package install/upgrade/removal, configuration edits, services/timers, firewall rules, etc. (`sudo -n` works without a password)
- GitHub operations: commits, pushes, branches, PRs, issues, workflow runs (via `gh`)
Only pause if credentials/2FA are required or the request itself is ambiguous.

# Instagram account monitoring

New posts are downloaded automatically by user-level systemd timers (hourly, images only, metadata removed afterward):
- pdm.clara → `pdm-clara-autodl.timer` (saved session)
- julie.tuzet → `julie-autodl.timer` (runs `ig-download julie.tuzet --new-only --session ivan_esteban23`)

Keep these timers enabled unless the user says otherwise. To add another account, create a matching service/timer pair using `ig-download <account> --new-only --session ivan_esteban23`.
Both timers poll the same session `ivan_esteban23`, so their hourly hits add up against one account. katarinabluu is manual-only (no timer); pull it with `ig-download katarinabluu --session ivan_esteban23` in tmux, in batches, when asked.

Timer scheduling note: use `OnCalendar=hourly` + `RemainAfterElapse=no`. A monotonic `OnBootSec`/`OnUnitActiveSec` pair can end up with `next_elapse=0` after a failed run, leaving the timer `active (elapsed)` with no next fire until it is restarted.

# Codex CLI sandbox (git/GitHub access)

`~/.codex/config.toml` keeps `sandbox_mode = "read-only"` and gives `[sandbox_workspace_write]` network access. Use `codex-git` (`~/.local/bin/codex-git`) instead of bare `codex` for any task that needs to push or merge: it exports `GH_TOKEN` from the gh keyring and layers `-c sandbox_mode=workspace-write -c sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true`. Repo writes and TMPDIR work; writes outside the workspace (e.g. `$HOME`) stay blocked. Verified: `gh api user`, `gh pr view`, `git ls-remote`, `git push`, SSH commit signing. Note `codex -p <profile> sandbox` loads the profile but ignores its `sandbox_mode`; only `-c` overrides take effect there.

`~/.local/bin/gh` falls back to the mise shim when `mise use` cannot write (read-only sandbox), because login shells put `~/.local/bin` ahead of the shims.
RULE

